 "stt": {
  "impl": "FasterWhisperSTT(unknown)",
  "rows": [
   {
    "sec": 2.002533100079745,
    "audio_sec": 8.568,
    "text": "Hi Arya, yes I am ready to start. I am a backend developer with three years of experience in Python and PostgreSQL."
   },
   {
    "sec": 2.7943043999839574,
    "audio_sec": 9.576,
    "text": "I built a pizza ordering app with Flask and PostgreSQL. It served around 200 users and handled orders, payments, and live order tracking."
   },
   {
    "sec": 2.016541099990718,
    "audio_sec": 8.928,
    "text": "For concurrency we use database transactions with row-level locking in post-grisql, so two users could not order the last pizza at the same time."
   },
   {
    "sec": 2.167108600027859,
    "audio_sec": 9.36,
    "text": "When the connection pull saturated during the lunch peak, I introduced PG Bonsor and added indexes on the orders table, which cut wait time by about 80%."
   },
   {
    "sec": 2.287477600038983,
    "audio_sec": 9.888,
    "text": "The main trade-off was consistency versus speed. I cached the menu and redis with a short expiry but always red stock levels directly from the database."
   },
   {
    "sec": 1.789492000010796,
    "audio_sec": 8.136,
    "text": "I would say my biggest weakness is system design at larger scale because most of my projects so far had under a thousand daily active users."
   },
   {
    "sec": 2.167456299997866,
    "audio_sec": 10.992,
    "text": "In a previous team we disagreed on using microservices. I proposed a modular monolith first, wrote a short design document, and the team agreed after a two week spike."
   },
   {
    "sec": 2.6008920000167564,
    "audio_sec": 8.712,
    "text": "For testing I wrote unit tests with Pytist for the pricing rules, and integration tests that spin up a temporary post-gress QL container for the order flow."
   }
  ]
 },
